Indeed, `Path(base_dir) / name` — if `name` contains `../` sequences, Path will resolve them. For example, `Path("/app/reports") / "../../etc/passwd"` becomes `/app/reports/../../etc/passwd`, and `write_text` will write to `/etc/passwd` (if permissions allow). There's no normalization, no `resolve()` check, no whitelist. This is a classic path traversal vulnerability.

Note: If `name` is an absolute path like `/etc/passwd`, `Path(base_dir) / "/etc/passwd"` actually returns `Path("/etc/passwd")` — Path's `/` operator discards the left side when the right side is absolute. So it's even worse.

